    "reason": "The English lemma 'substance' has multiple common meanings, including 'physical material' (e.g., 'a strange substance') and 'essence/core meaning' (e.g., 'the substance of an argument'). The Japanese expression '実質' (じっしつ) primarily covers the 'essence/core meaning' of substance, as indicated by its JMdict glosses ['substance', 'essence']. However, it does not cover the 'physical material' meaning, for which '物質' (ぶっしつ) would be more appropriate. Therefore, '実質' is semantically narrower than the full scope of the English lemma 'substance'. This narrowness makes it potentially misleading for a general learner who might encounter 'substance' in its physical sense, thus reducing its naturalness as a sole equivalent for the broad English lemma.",
